## Context

Undergraduate thesis, June 2025: *Design of Thermal Comfort Monitoring Information System with Rapid Application Development Method*. The system supports indoor health, ergonomics, and productivity by continuously monitoring room environmental conditions.

## Problem

Evaluating indoor thermal comfort manually with standalone meters only captures point-in-time snapshots, leaving no continuous historical record, no trend visibility, and no timely alert when conditions drift outside comfort thresholds.

## My role

Core team (System Analyst & Developer): I designed the information system architecture, data schema, and RAD web dashboard connecting the IoT sensor stream to real-time visualization, historical logging, and user-configurable alert settings.

## As-is analysis

Manual environmental checks required periodic on-site measurements and manual spreadsheet logging, making it slow to detect thermal comfort drops or analyze patterns across time for academic research and room operations.

## Requirements

- Continuous real-time ingestion of four environmental parameters: temperature, humidity, air quality, and wind speed.
- Web-based dashboard displaying live readings alongside stored historical data and time-trend graphs.
- User personalization for alert notification thresholds and data synchronization frequency.
- Fast iterative delivery using Rapid Application Development (RAD).

## Solution design

The architecture connects an Arduino MKR Wi-Fi 1010 sensor device over Wi-Fi to a PHP/MySQL web server. Following Rapid Application Development (RAD), the dashboard and alert workflows were prototyped iteratively with user feedback so readings remained clear and actionable.

## Build

A full-stack information system built with Arduino MKR Wi-Fi 1010, HTML/CSS/JavaScript, PHP, and MySQL (`monitoring-termal-demo.onrender.com`), presenting live sensor streams, historical trend charts, and configurable threshold notifications. Source code: [github.com/thunderclouds863/monitoring-kenyamanan-termal](https://github.com/thunderclouds863/monitoring-kenyamanan-termal).

## Verification

Validated end-to-end sensor-to-dashboard data transmission, historical log storage, trend visualization, and threshold alert triggers during thesis testing and defense.

## Outcome

Passed the undergraduate thesis defense without revision, and the computer program (*Monitoring Kenyamanan Termal*) was registered under Indonesian copyright law (`EC002025076498`, Record No. `000916759`, 26 June 2025; copyright holder: Universitas Diponegoro; Desi Armanda Sari listed as creator #6 among 8 contributors).

## Reflection

Combining hardware telemetry with a clean RAD web interface showed how important configurable thresholds and sync intervals are for turning raw sensor readings into practical operational decisions.
